#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[ABOUT THIS BOOKLET])
#v(0.08in)

Blaise Pascal (1623-1662) was a French mathematician and physicist, the
builder of one of the first calculating machines. After a night of
conversion in 1654 he gave his last years to a defense of the Christian
faith that he never finished. What he left were bundles of notes, some a
single line, some several pages. They were published after his death as the
_Pensées_, the "Thoughts."

The fragments gathered here circle one idea: _divertissement_, diversion.
For Pascal the word is wider than entertainment. It is anything that turns
our attention away from ourselves: the hunt, the game, the war, the office,
the endless round of business. We do not seek these things for what they
are, he says, but for the noise they make, because a quiet room leaves us
alone with our condition, and we cannot bear it.

This booklet is a companion to No. 01. Aquinas named the restless craving to
see and to know; Pascal asks why we flee into it. Both were written long
before the infinite scroll. Both describe it.

#pagebreak()
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[HOW TO READ THE FRAGMENTS])
#v(0.08in)

Pascal left notes, not a finished book, so read them as notes: one at a
time, slowly, letting each one land before the next.

*The numbers* are those of Léon Brunschvicg's edition (1897), which Trotter's
translation follows. Other editions arrange the fragments differently and
number them differently.

*Small headings* in italics, such as _Diversion_, are Pascal's own labels.

*Square brackets* [ ] mark passages that Pascal himself crossed out in his
manuscript but did not replace.

*The order* is the editor's. Part One describes our restless condition; Part
Two, diversion, the remedy we invent; Part Three, the remedy Pascal proposes.

Pascal read Montaigne's _Essays_ closely and borrows from them often; the
hunter, the gambler and the king all appear there first.
